# ForWebClaude — Capability-Parity Spec

**Audience:** the WebClaude agent (a browser-based Claude Code work-alike).
**Author:** Claude Code, running on Zack's Kali box, inside the MehhSpace repo.
**Goal:** tell you (WebClaude) everything you need to reproduce the capabilities I
have from the Claude Code CLI — especially **running MCP servers** — so that a
project which "only works in Claude Code" also works from WebClaude.

This is written for the concrete MehhSpace case, but the framework in §1–§4 and §7
is **project-agnostic on purpose.** The whole point is that after you self-update,
you shouldn't hit this wall again on the next project.

> How to use this file: read §1 for the mental model, apply the checklist in §2,
> wire MCPs per §3, solve viewing per §4, then use §5 as a worked example and §6 as
> the concrete to-do for MehhSpace. §7 is the future-proofing convention — adopt it.

---

## 1. The core idea — why a project feels "Claude Code only"

A Claude Code project has **no magic**. Its capabilities are just the union of a few
layers. A project feels "CLI-only" when WebClaude is missing one or more of them —
almost never all of them. Reproduce the layers and the gap closes.

The layers:

| Layer | What it is | Where it lives |
|---|---|---|
| **Execution host** | the machine that actually runs Bash / reads / writes files | must be the box that holds the repo + creds |
| **Toolchain** | `node`, `npm`, `git`, `gh`, `lsof`, etc. on that host | system packages |
| **Auth / credentials** | git push rights, API tokens, gh/ssh keys | host config (`~/.config/gh`, ssh agent, env) |
| **Slash-commands** | `.claude/commands/*.md` — a description, `allowed-tools`, and a prose procedure | in the repo |
| **Skills** | `.claude/skills/*` — reusable capability bundles | in the repo / user dir |
| **MCP servers** | external tool providers (Notion, Gmail, Drive…) exposed as `mcp__<server>__*` tools | agent config, not the repo |
| **Network reachability** | being able to *view* a local dev server from a browser | LAN / tunnel |

**Key realization:** the slash-commands and skills already travel *with the repo*.
If WebClaude loads `.claude/commands/` and runs on the right host, it inherits those
for free. The two layers that usually don't travel with the repo — and that you must
provision — are **MCP servers** and **network reachability (viewing)**. Everything
else is "is my Bash executing on the box that has the repo + auth?"

